@*
    DesktopStatusBar.razor
    Purpose: The always-visible "Connected to ..." chip and its "Change connection" action.

    Mockups: 40-blogapp-login.html (the connected-to row above the sign-in form) and
    41-blogapp-shell.html (the connection badge in the admin topbar). Both ask for the same
    thing — the desktop head must always show which database it is pointed at, because unlike
    the website it can be aimed anywhere.

    Why it lives here rather than in the pages: BlogApp reuses BlogUI's LoginPage, AuthLayout
    and AdminLayout unchanged (REQ-UI-052 — one RCL, two heads). Rendering the chip once,
    above the router, keeps every shared page byte-for-byte identical to the website while
    still giving the desktop head its connection affordance on every screen.

    UAT-020: this used to be anchored `fixed bottom-3 left-3`, which floated on top of the
    admin sidebar's lowest nav entry (Settings) once the sidebar was expanded — a real menu
    item became unreachable. It is now a real, in-flow TOP banner: because ConnectionGuard
    renders it as a sibling BEFORE the router's output, it sits above every shared layout
    (AdminLayout, AuthLayout, DesktopSetupLayout) and pushes their content down rather than
    floating over it — none of those layouts needed a desktop-specific edit for this. `sticky
    top-0` keeps it reachable if the window's content ever grows tall enough to scroll (this
    head is a WebView2 with no browser chrome, so a banner that scrolled out of view would be
    unreachable), while still reserving its own space in normal flow so nothing beneath it —
    sidebar entries included — is ever covered. `z-40` is intentionally below the auth/setup
    screens' fixed top-right theme toggle (z-50) so that control always stays on top and
    clickable if the two ever share a corner at narrow widths.
*@

@inject ConnectionContext ConnectionContext
@inject NavigationManager NavigationManager

@if (ConnectionContext.IsConfigured)
{
    <div class="sticky top-0 z-40 flex w-full items-center justify-center gap-2 border-b border-border
                bg-background/95 px-3 py-1.5 text-xs text-muted-foreground shadow-sm"
         data-testid="desktop-connection-status">
        <span class="flex h-2 w-2 rounded-full bg-primary" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>Connected</span>
        <span class="font-mono text-foreground" data-testid="desktop-connection-host">@ConnectionContext.DisplayLabel</span>
        <Button Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost"
                Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                OnClick="OpenConnectionSettings"
                data-testid="desktop-change-connection">
            Change connection
        </Button>
    </div>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Opens the connection-settings screen.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Navigates rather than linking so the chip behaves the same inside the admin shell,
    /// where an anchor would inherit the sidebar's active-link styling.
    /// </remarks>
    private void OpenConnectionSettings()
    {
        NavigationManager.NavigateTo(ConnectionSetup.SettingsRoute);
    }
}
